```apex
/**
 * FollowUpTaskService
 *
 * Creates a standard follow-up Task for each Opportunity Id passed in, owned by
 * the Opportunity's owner, due 7 days from today. Designed for the weekly
 * pipeline review (150-250 Opportunities passed in a single call).
 *
 * Failure isolation:
 *   Compliance validation rules can reject individual Tasks. A rejected Task
 *   never blocks the others, and every Opportunity whose Task could not be
 *   created is returned in the resulting Set<Id> (empty when all succeeded).
 *
 * Implementation note (intentional deviation from a per-record insert loop):
 *   The review volume (150-250) exceeds the 150-DML-statements-per-transaction
 *   governor limit, so inserting one Task per loop iteration would throw an
 *   uncatchable System.LimitException around record 151 and roll back every
 *   follow-up created so far. Instead, Tasks are inserted with
 *   Database.insert(..., allOrNone = false) in chunks, which preserves the
 *   required semantics exactly: each record is evaluated independently,
 *   validation rejections are isolated to their own record, and failed records
 *   are mapped back to their Opportunity Ids via SaveResult.
 */
public with sharing class FollowUpTaskService {

    /** DML chunk size: 250 opportunities -> 2 DML statements; scales well past review volume. */
    private static final Integer DML_CHUNK_SIZE = 200;
    private static final Integer FOLLOW_UP_IN_DAYS = 7;
    private static final String TASK_SUBJECT_PREFIX = 'Follow up: ';
    private static final String TASK_STATUS_NOT_STARTED = 'Not Started';
    private static final String TASK_PRIORITY_NORMAL = 'Normal';

    /**
     * Creates a follow-up Task for each given Opportunity.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds Opportunity Ids from the weekly pipeline review;
     *        duplicates and nulls are ignored.
     * @return Ids of the Opportunities for which no Task could be created
     *         (validation rules, inactive owners, unresolvable Ids, ...).
     *         Empty when every Task was created.
     */
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // Deduplicate and drop nulls so one Id cannot produce duplicate follow-ups.
        Set<Id> requestedIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id opportunityId : opportunityIds) {
            if (opportunityId != null) {
                requestedIds.add(opportunityId);
            }
        }
        if (requestedIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // Resolve everything in one query (never query inside the loop).
        Map<Id, Opportunity> opportunities = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId
             FROM Opportunity
             WHERE Id IN :requestedIds]
        );

        Date dueDate = Date.today().addDays(FOLLOW_UP_IN_DAYS);

        // Build the Tasks plus a parallel list mapping each Task to its Opportunity,
        // so failed SaveResults can be attributed back to the right Id.
        List<Task> followUpTasks = new List<Task>();
        List<Id> taskToOpportunity = new List<Id>();

        for (Id opportunityId : requestedIds) {
            Opportunity opp = opportunities.get(opportunityId);
            if (opp == null) {
                // Deleted, wrong object type, or not visible to the running user:
                // no Task can be created, so report it rather than attempting a doomed DML.
                failedOpportunityIds.add(opportunityId);
                continue;
            }
            followUpTasks.add(new Task(
                WhatId       = opp.Id,
                OwnerId      = opp.OwnerId,
                Subject      = TASK_SUBJECT_PREFIX + opp.Name,
                ActivityDate = dueDate,
                Status       = TASK_STATUS_NOT_STARTED,
                Priority     = TASK_PRIORITY_NORMAL
            ));
            taskToOpportunity.add(opportunityId);
        }

        // Partial-success DML in chunks: one rejected Task never blocks the others.
        for (Integer startIndex = 0; startIndex < followUpTasks.size(); startIndex += DML_CHUNK_SIZE) {
            Integer endIndex = Math.min(startIndex + DML_CHUNK_SIZE, followUpTasks.size());
            List<Task> chunk = followUpTasks.subList(startIndex, endIndex);

            List<Database.SaveResult> results;
            try {
                results = Database.insert(chunk, false);
            } catch (DmlException unexpected) {
                // Safety net for non-record-specific DML failures (e.g. mixed-DML
                // contexts): attribute the whole chunk as failed instead of aborting
                // the run, so the rest of the review still gets its follow-ups.
                for (Integer i = startIndex; i < endIndex; i++) {
                    failedOpportunityIds.add(taskToOpportunity.get(i));
                }
                logFailure(taskToOpportunity.get(startIndex),
                    'Unexpected DML failure affecting chunk of ' + chunk.size()
                    + ' task(s): ' + unexpected.getMessage());
                continue;
            }

            for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
                if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                    Id failedOpportunityId = taskToOpportunity.get(startIndex + i);
                    failedOpportunityIds.add(failedOpportunityId);
                    List<String> messages = new List<String>();
                    for (Database.Error err : results[i].getErrors()) {
                        messages.add(err.getStatusCode() + ': ' + err.getMessage());
                    }
                    logFailure(failedOpportunityId, String.join(messages, '; '));
                }
            }
        }

        return failedOpportunityIds;
    }

    /** Writes rejection reasons to the debug log so compliance rejections are diagnosable. */
    private static void logFailure(Id opportunityId, String message) {
        System.debug(LoggingLevel.WARN,
            'Follow-up task could not be created for Opportunity ' + opportunityId
            + ' -> ' + message);
    }
}
```

**What I did differently from the literal "loop and insert each on its own" pattern, and why:**

1. **No per-record DML — chunked `Database.insert(chunk, false)` instead.** This is the critical one: your stated volume (150–250 opportunities) sits right on top of the **150 DML statements per transaction** governor limit. A literal one-insert-per-iteration loop throws an uncatchable `System.LimitException` at opportunity 151, which aborts the whole run and rolls back *every* follow-up created so far — the exact opposite of "a rejected task must not stop the others." With `Database.insert(..., allOrNone = false)`, every task is still attempted independently, a compliance validation rejection on one record never touches the others, and the observable contract is identical: the Set of opportunity Ids whose tasks failed. At 250 records this costs 2 DML statements instead of 250. I chunk at 200 rather than a single call so the method also degrades gracefully if the review grows into the thousands (chunking doesn't defeat the 10,000-rows-per-transaction cap, though — the method is intended for review-sized batches).

2. **Failure detection via `SaveResult` instead of `try/catch`.** Partial-success DML gives me per-record success/failure, so failures map back to the correct opportunity Id precisely, and I get the actual validation status codes/messages (debug-logged at WARN) — a per-record catch gives you a generic exception with no record context.

3. **One SOQL, not a query in the loop.** The naive loop pattern usually also queries the opportunity per iteration, which would break the 100-SOQL limit the same way. I resolve all Ids in a single query; Ids that don't resolve (deleted, wrong object type, or not visible to the running user) go straight into the returned failure set rather than attempting a doomed insert. The class is `with sharing`, so records the user can't see fail loudly and visibly instead of being written through a privilege escalation.

4. **Input hygiene:** duplicates and nulls are dropped so a repeated Id can't create two identical follow-ups.

5. **Subject built exactly per spec** (`'Follow up: ' + Name`). If a Name is long enough to overflow the 255-char Subject field, that specific insert fails and its Id lands in the returned set — consistent with "compliance rejects some."

Two deployment notes: you'll still need a test class for production (success path, partial failure — e.g., via a Task validation rule or forced DML failure — and unresolvable Ids); and the hardcoded `'Not Started'` / `'Normal'` assume the standard picklist values survive — if the org ever renames them, the affected records surface as failures per the contract, or the constants can be made describe-driven.

